    invocation: "Life is a movie, and yours is playing right now, full of the drama and betrayal and comedy and devastating loss you binge on screens, the things we assume belong to fictional people in fictional worlds.\n\nThe stories that wreck you on screen wreck you because they are already in you. The grief you feel watching a stranger's loss is your grief. The hope you feel at the end is your hope. The screen is the mirror. Your life is the thing being reflected.\n\nYour life has car chases, love scenes, and villain monologues. The quiet moment before the battle. The ugly cry in the parking lot. The laugh that saves someone's life. Doors that open, and doors that close.\n\nAnd the soundtrack swelling underneath, at the exact moment the character decides to stop running and turn around and face the thing.\n\nYour brain is a story engine that treats narrative as reality. That is why you watch a film and cry. The tears are real, because some old part of you experiences the story as reality itself. The brain runs the story and produces the chemistry, faithfully, every time. So if narrative is reality to the brain, then reality is your narrative. Your life is a movie. And if story is that real to you, then your life is the most important movie ever made.\n\nYou have just been sitting so close to the screen that you forgot you were the one directing it all.\n\nEvery director knows the secret is the frame. Point the camera tight on the spilled coffee and the scene is a tragedy. Widen the shot to the whole kitchen, the dog skidding through the puddle, and it becomes a comedy. Pull back far enough, to the years before and the years after, and the same moment becomes one beat in an epic. Nothing in the scene changes. The frame changes everything.\n\nThat is framing: the art of deciding what the camera sees and what it means. The frame sets the context, the context shifts the perspective, and the perspective makes the story. It is the first art, because every other art depends on what you choose to look at. And this chapter is where you learn to turn it on your own life.\n\nFilm carries one more gift, hidden inside its biggest limitation. The camera cannot go inside anyone's head. It can only show what people do: where they go, what they say, what they reach for, what they walk away from. So that is the discipline of this whole chapter: stay outside. Describe this character the way a camera would. The actions, the scenes, the visible life. Everything the camera cannot see comes in the next chapter, in the form that was invented to hold it. For now, the distance is the gift.\n\nIt asks four things of you. Watch the movie. See the character. See the whole plot. And tell the movie.",
    transition: "You have a film now. The scenes, the character, the plot, the whole arc visible for the first time. And you told everything the camera can see.\n\nEverything the camera cannot see is next. The footage you have been cutting from every draft. The scenes too dark or too raw for the room. Good. Every director knows the best footage is the footage that scares them.\n\nThat walk inward is what turns a decent film into the kind of story that changes a life.",
